{
  "id": "hksbcf0",
  "title": "Chapter 7: Electricity and Magnetism",
  "order": 56,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lghhivb",
      "content": "Electricity and magnetism are closely related branches of physics. Electricity deals with the flow of electric charges, while magnetism deals with the magnetic properties of materials and magnetic fields produced by electric currents."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Electric Current Unit",
          "value": "Ampere (A)",
          "icon": "zap"
        },
        {
          "label": "Ohm's Law",
          "value": "V = I × R",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Electric Power Unit",
          "value": "Watt (W)",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Resistance Unit",
          "value": "Ohm (Ω)",
          "icon": "info"
        }
      ],
      "id": "qkk9nau"
    },
    {
      "id": "jqnvh79",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Electricity"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Electric current is defined as the rate of flow of electric charge through a conductor.",
        "Ohm's Law establishes that potential difference is directly proportional to current at constant temperature (V = IR).",
        "Electric power represents the rate of electrical energy consumption per unit time (P = VI).",
        "Magnetic poles always exist in pairs (North and South); like poles repel, unlike poles attract.",
        "An electromagnet generates a temporary magnetic field when electric current flows through its coils."
      ],
      "id": "r5jquh4"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hk9qae7",
      "content": "Electricity is the flow of electric charge through a conductor. Electric current is the movement of electrons through a conductor such as copper wire."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kbnmrad",
      "content": "• Electric charge is a fundamental property of matter."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nkugub8",
      "content": "• Electric current is the flow of electric charge."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mlpoqvl",
      "content": "• The SI unit of electric current is Ampere (A)."
    },
    {
      "id": "uw9csla",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Electric Current"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "w2z36lz",
      "content": "Electric current is defined as the rate of flow of electric charge."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s5as5u7",
      "content": "Formula: I = Q / t"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "en3nqdh",
      "content": "Where:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "fl84x6h",
      "content": "I = Electric current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "sxjv83i",
      "content": "Q = Electric charge"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ik2pkit",
      "content": "t = Time"
    },
    {
      "id": "ve3b017",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Voltage"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hu7jk92",
      "content": "Voltage is the potential difference between two points in an electric circuit."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4c7gvrg",
      "content": "SI Unit of Voltage = Volt (V)"
    },
    {
      "id": "tygkk2h",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Resistance"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Electricity and Magnetism",
      "id": "ommfken"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1uls43l",
      "content": "Resistance is the opposition offered by a conductor to the flow of electric current."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yll0jhd",
      "content": "SI Unit of Resistance = Ohm (Ω)"
    },
    {
      "id": "zix2mye",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Ohm’s Law"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4hwqhfe",
      "content": "Ohm’s Law states that the current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the voltage across it, provided temperature remains constant."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "t6tsuyd",
      "content": "Formula: V = IR"
    },
    {
      "id": "iqaehs7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Electric Power"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hteut82",
      "content": "Electric power is the rate at which electrical energy is consumed."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "6yzj166",
      "content": "Formula: P = VI"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "sb0gmhz",
      "content": "SI Unit = Watt (W)"
    },
    {
      "id": "vp6rq6a",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Types of Electric Circuits"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Series Circuit",
        "Parallel Circuit"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Current Flow",
          "Same through all components",
          "Divides across parallel branches"
        ],
